      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "An overview is presented of the physiology and so-called pathophysiology of static muscle contractions in the intact living organism. Static muscle activity produces no external mechanophysical work, but is used for fixation. Contingent upon the levels of the generated forces, and the duration of the isometric contractions, static activities give rise to fatigue and pains. The discomforts are viewed as somatic responses that are normally within physiological limits. That is, upon cessation of isometric contractions there is usually rapid recovery from the discomforts, and they do not normally induce chronic lesions."},
